	affiliations = {University Tripura Campus, Department of Cyber Security and Digital Forensics, India; Malla Reddy University, Department of Digital Forensics, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {Cloud computing services can be categorized into software-as-a-service (SaaS), platform as a service (PaaS), and infrastructure as a service (IaaS). Security is the responsibility of both providers and clients. Cloud concepts include public, private, community, and hybrid cloud deployments. Security vulnerabilities in cloud computing systems have been studied, with research focusing on data, application, network, and host levels. Researchers have developed ECC-based multi-server authentication, Open Stack platform models, iHAC access control architecture, network-level solutions, dynamic proof approaches, and Open Pipe SaaS. Data security and privacy are critical as businesses migrate to the Internet-based cloud computing paradigm. Infrastructure gaps such as hypervisor security, third-party auditing, and data protection must be addressed. Advanced technology like blockchain and computational intelligence is needed for modern security. Context-aware solutions, third-party auditing without data access, and network security measures like HTTPS, TLS, and HTTP request anomaly detection are essential. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
