	affiliations = {University of Information Science and Technology (UIST) “St. Paul the Apostle”, Ohrid, North Macedonia},
	abstract = {As cloud computing continues to rapidly emerge and evolve, both in conceptualization and practical implementation, significant challenges persist in the realms of legal, service quality, interoperability, security, and privacy. However, these modern advancements come with inherent risks and uncertainties, mirroring the nature of cloud computing itself. Essentially, organizations heavily reliant on information technology opt to outsource their operations, often engaging in crucial transactions. This reliance exposes them to significant threats, especially when sensitive analytical data is at risk of exposure due to inadequate security measures. While legal challenges vary across different countries, there is a widespread expectation that not every organization will be granted authorization to utilize public clouds. Consequently, a surge in the adoption of private clouds is anticipated. In the ensuing discussion, we will briefly delve into the ongoing relevance of privacy, security, and legal issues for users. This analysis highlights the pressing need to address numerous legal challenges in future research on cloud computing. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
