	affiliations = {Chandigarh Group of Colleges Jhanjeri, Department of Computer Application, Mohali, India; Graphic Era Hill University, Department of Management Studies, Dehradun, India; The Islamic University, Najaf, Department of Computers Techniques Engineering, Najaf, Iraq; "Tashkent Institute of Irrigation and Agricultural Mechanization Engineers" National Research University, Department of Chemistry and Physics, Tashkent, Uzbekistan; Western Caspian University Baku, Baku, Azerbaijan; GLA University, Mathura, Department of Electrical Engineering, Mathura, India; Graphic Era Deemed to be University, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Dehradun, India},
	abstract = {It is clear that cloud use is growing quickly across a number of different companies and institutions. Cloud data storage is one of the many benefits of cloud computing that is most noticeable. Under this paradigm, data owners choose to store most of their data on cloud services instead of own servers. The sensitivity of the data and the need for prompt information processing make cloud security an essential factor to take into account. In order to improve data security and confidentiality, this study presents a novel security architecture. Data is arranged into blocks of bits under the suggested security architecture. Every two bits, a genetic algorithm is implemented as part of a revolutionary security architecture that segments these bits. Every time the genetic algorithm runs, a ciphertext made up of two-bit blocks is produced. Interestingly, every ciphertext has a unique home in a different part of the cloud, and its location is always changing, making it difficult for any enemies to figure out where it is. Furthermore, one important way that genetic algorithms support data security is by not requiring the presence of a fundamental idea. Using evolutionary algorithms on lower block sizes, the new architecture strengthens security measures even more. The capability list is an essential part of this system that provides extra tools for safe and fine-grained data access. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
