	year = {2023},
	pages = {748 - 751},
	doi = {10.1109/PEEIC59336.2023.10450434},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85188059860&doi=10.1109%2FPEEIC59336.2023.10450434&partnerID=40&md5=5e819be2bcbded8816a9bced46bdf894},
	affiliations = {Presidency University Bangalore, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Bengaluru, India; JAIN (Deemed-to-be University), Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Bengaluru, India; Maharishi University of Information Technology, Maharishi School of Engineering & Technology, Lucknow, India},
	abstract = {The use of online computing has grown quickly across a range of institutions and businesses. The storage of information in the clouds, which enables individuals and businesses to store their personal information largely in the cloud and not on local computers, is one of the main benefits of cloud computing. Safety of data maintained in the cloud is still a major worry, however. We provide a novel security architecture in this study that is intended to improve data confidentiality and safety. Data is separated into blocks of two bytes each, and each multi-byte bit sequence is subjected to a separate evolutionary process in the revised security framework. The end product of each genetic algorithm's operation is ciphertext, which is made up of two different sets of bits. The ciphertext for these encrypted messages is difficult to find since it is stored in many places across the cloud. Additionally, the newly developed safety architecture uses algorithms that evolve on fewer data blocks to improve data security. A capability list is also used to guarantee granular and secure access to information. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
