	affiliations = {University of Hyderabad, Hyderabad, India; Institute for Development and Research in Banking Technology India, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {The Internet of Things (IoT) is a major technological advancement in which every device is connected to the internet. Authentication and access control protocols must be robust enough to enable security in layered architectures of Internet of Things. Considering there are many sensor node devices frequently joining and leaving the network. Therefore, traditional authentication and key agreement schemes are not suitable for these dynamic networks. Many real-time applications make use of these networks, so that the number of mobile and sensing devices might increase exponentially in the near future. In parallel, the number of attacks on these devices also increased, such as denial of service (DoS), impersonation, man-in-the-middle and replay attacks. In this paper, we propose an Elliptic Curve Cryptography(ECC)based anonymous authenticated protocol for the Internet of Things(IoT) environment that enables the mutual authentication between IoT enabled client devices and cloud servers. An identity-based authentication is used in the proposed protocol in which an IoT client device is granted access to use service at a cloud server only after the successful presentation of two or more pieces of evidence or factors for an authentication mechanism. The proposed scheme's security is checked against known IoT network attacks using the industry-standard verification and validation tool, Automated Validation of Internet Security Protocols and Applications (AVISPA). © 2023 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
