	affiliations = {Pranveer Singh Institute of Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Kanpur, India; Amity University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Noida, India},
	abstract = {The history of IoT-integrated cloud and data solutions can be traced back to the early days of IoT and cloud computing. As IoT devices became more prevalent, the need to efficiently manage and analyze a lot of information they generated led to the development of these solutions. The early solutions mainly focused on storing and processing data, but as technology has advanced, they have evolved to include more advanced analytics and machine learning capabilities. However, despite their benefits, IoT-integrated cloud and data solutions also face several challenges. One major issue is security, as the IoT’s significant data output devices can make them a target for cyberattacks. Additionally, there is also difficulty with data privacy, as the data assembled by IoT devices can include exposed data that has to be safeguarded. Another challenge is information management, often known as the sheer volume and variety of data launched by IoT devices can be overwhelming. This can be addressed by using innovative machine learning and analytics tools to make sense of the data, but these tools can be complex and require specialized expertise to operate effectively. Furthermore, integrating IoT devices and their data with other systems and data sources can also be a challenge, as different systems may use different protocols and data formats. This can require significant effort and resources to implement. Finally, the cost of implementing and maintaining IoT-integrated cloud and data solutions can also be high, making it a barrier for small and medium-sized businesses. All these challenges require the implementation of robust security measures and the use of progressive technologies to guarantee the confidentiality and safety of data, make the data manageable, and integrate it with other systems and data sources. This chapter aims at how an Internet of Things (IoT)-integrated cloud and data solution is used in a system that uses the Internet of Things to link tangible objects and objects to the cloud, where data launched by these devices is summed up, refined, and stored. This allows for the assortment and thorough study of massive data sets from IoT devices, enabling the evolution of new requisitions and amenities as well as the extraction of valuable insights. Components of an IoT-integrated cloud and data may include IoT devices, cloud infrastructure, data processing, analytics tools, and applications and services. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
