	abstract = {This study proposes a theoretical model explaining the security factors that determine user intentions to choose a cloud technology service, especially on Software as a Service. By understanding the antecedents of using cloud technology software as a service, users will become more alert and careful in choosing cloud technology software as a service in the future. Knowledge of security factors in cloud technology services in the current era is fundamental and significantly impacts data and information used by anyone and anywhere. The D&M model use and data were tested through a structural equation approach and the Partial Least Square (PLS) technique with snowball sampling as a data collection technique. The study was conducted with 424 people who had previously used cloud technology software services for 12 months, of which 273 were male (64.4%), and 151 were female (35.6%). The study's conclusion revealed that the intention to use software services on cloud technology is influenced directly or indirectly by the warranty, including the License of the Software used. (License), antivirus, device usage records (Log History), then the information given to users (Notification) and access control (Access Control), as well as authenticity of user identity (Authentication User). © 2022 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
